# tools/ales_stereo_ring_mod_witness.rish — stereo_ring_mod: the ring modulator (bipolar triangle carrier on amplitude) carried into stereo, the same carrier on both channels — the second rung of the stereo modulation class (ALES177).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES177.
# Design read: active-designing/20260815-083200_fill-ales177-lotus-stereo-ring-mod.md
# Stands on: lotus/ring_mod.rye (ALES75 — the mono ring modulator run once per channel, its RingModError reused whole and its precheck reused for the up-front atomic validation), lotus/tremolo.rye (ALES74 — the non-negative-carrier
# equality target, carried into stereo at ALES176), lotus/glide.rye (ALES70 — glide_scale, 256 = unity), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip and the one true saturate).
#
# lotus/stereo_ring_mod.rye clangs a stereo master: it runs ALES75's proven mono ring modulator on BOTH channels with the SAME carrier — a bipolar triangle of depth round centre over period samples multiplying each sample. The ring
# modulator is the tremolo with one restriction lifted: the carrier may cross zero and go negative, flipping the sample's sign and scattering the spectrum into metallic sum-and-difference tones; a ring modulator whose carrier never
# goes negative is exactly the tremolo. The same carrier on both channels is the linked stereo ring modulator, both speakers clanging together. A carrier is scaled units the caller names (256 = unity), not scalars measured across the
# field, so the same carrier on each channel preserves the image for free; because each output is its own input times the shared carrier c(k), an identical-channel master stays identical and a panned master keeps its pan exactly, the
# left:right ratio untouched even when c(k) turns negative and flips both signs together. Like the tremolo and unlike a delay-line modulation, every output depends only on its own input sample, so it writes in place with no snapshot;
# the asymmetric i16 rail's lone inverted corner saturates to 32767 per channel. It validates the carrier and span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated (BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadCarrier, BadRange), so a
# refusal never rings one channel and leaves the other torn; RingModError reused whole. Values only, never a length; the two channels leave balanced. The crux is four laws: the stereo ring law (each channel equal to ALES75's mono
# ring_mod with the same carrier byte for byte, the channels genuinely differing), the tremolo-sibling law (a non-negative carrier equal to ALES176's stereo tremolo on both, a bipolar carrier genuinely differing), the image law (an
# identical-channel master staying identical, a panned pair keeping its ratio through sign flips), and the balance / silence / atomicity / degenerate law.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_ring_mod_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-ring-mod: Language EN — stereo_ring_mod clangs a stereo master by running ALES75's proven mono ring modulator on both channels with the same carrier: a bipolar triangle of depth round centre over period samples multiplying each sample. The ring modulator is the tremolo with one restriction lifted, the carrier allowed to cross zero and go negative, flipping the sample's sign and scattering the spectrum into metallic sum-and-difference tones; a ring modulator whose carrier never goes negative is exactly the tremolo. The same carrier on both channels is the linked stereo ring modulator, both speakers clanging together. A carrier is scaled units the caller names, 256 being unity, not scalars measured across the field, so the same carrier on each channel preserves the image for free; because each output is its own input times the shared carrier c of k, an identical-channel master stays identical and a panned master keeps its pan exactly, the left to right ratio untouched even when the carrier turns negative and flips both signs together. Like the tremolo and unlike a delay-line modulation, every output depends only on its own input sample, so it writes in place with no snapshot. It validates the carrier and span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never rings one channel and leaves the other torn; it writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced."
say "ales-stereo-ring-mod: Style Radiant — ALES176 opened the stereo modulation class with the tremolo, a triangle oscillator on the gain held to an attenuation so the carrier never crosses zero and the write's saturate is a proven no-op. Its named sibling is that rung with one restriction lifted: the carrier may cross zero and go negative, flipping the sample's sign; swung fast it multiplies the signal by a bipolar wave and scatters the spectrum into metallic sum-and-difference tones, the ring modulator's unmistakable clang. Because a ring modulator whose carrier never goes negative is exactly the tremolo, carrying it into stereo is the natural second modulation rung, the same thin per-sample discipline with one bound widened. This rung runs ALES75's proven ring modulator on each channel with the same carrier, so both speakers clang together and the image is held; because each output is its own input times the shared carrier, an identical-channel master stays identical and a panned master keeps its pan exactly, both sides multiplied by the same carrier so the left to right ratio at every sample is untouched even where the carrier turns negative and flips both signs together. The one honest subtlety the tremolo never met carries too: the two's-complement rail is asymmetric, sample minimum being minus 32768, so a carrier at minus unity on a full-negative sample lands plus 32768, one past the maximum, and the one true saturate clamps that lone corner to 32767 rather than wrapping, per channel. The crux is four laws: the stereo ring law, each channel equal to ALES75's mono ring modulator with the same carrier byte for byte with the two channels genuinely differing so no channel is crossed; the tremolo-sibling law, a non-negative carrier equal to ALES176's stereo tremolo on both channels byte for byte since the two rungs are one multiply with the sign the only difference, and a bipolar carrier genuinely differing as a sign the tremolo can never produce; the image law, an identical-channel master staying identical through the ring modulator and a panned pair keeping its left to right ratio at every sample through the sign flips; and the balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate law, an all-silent master ringing to silence on both with the lengths held, a bad period, a bad depth, a bad carrier, and an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, and count zero the identity on both."
say "ales-stereo-ring-mod: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 clips (left, right), each rung through ALES75's own unit-bounded bipolar carrier, the triangle and multiply run in i64 and the write saturating once, a no-op except the lone sample-minimum-inverted corner where it genuinely earns its keep; the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a ring modulator writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit); the carrier and span are validated once against the shared length before either channel is mutated (ALES75's precheck, factored out of the mono ring modulator and shared, mirroring the ALES176 tremolo refactor), so a refusal never rings one channel and leaves the other torn; StereoRingModError is ALES75's RingModError (BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadCarrier, BadRange) reused whole, the stereo lift adding no fault. The stereo ring law is proven by running both stereo_ring_mod and ALES75's mono ring modulator on the same per-channel content and demanding byte-for-byte equality on both channels while the two channels genuinely differ; the tremolo-sibling law proves a non-negative carrier equals ALES176's stereo tremolo on both channels byte for byte and a bipolar carrier diverges from it; the image law proves an identical-channel master stays identical and a right-is-half-left pan keeps its ratio sample for sample through the sign flips; and the balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove an all-silent master staying silent on both, the length held, each of the four refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the carrier in scaled units rather than hertz, no delay line, no snapshot, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_ring_mod"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_ring_mod.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-ring-mod: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_ring_mod.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-ring-mod: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-ring-mod: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-ring-mod' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-ring-mod: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-ring-mod line"
